.page omikuji 1 draw today's shrine fortune slip

# SYNOPSIS
*omikuji* [*-rak*]

# DESCRIPTION
At a shrine you shake a box until a numbered stick falls out and the maiden hands you the matching slip. `omikuji` does it for you: it names a rank, from *Dai-kichi* (great blessing) down to *Kyo* (misfortune), and gives a line each for your wish, travel, business, health, lost items and love.

The slip is the same all day: it is drawn from the user id and the (UTC) date, so asking again at lunch does not improve it. Different users get different slips. Use `-r` to draw again, if you must.

The ranks, from best to worst, and how often they come up:

    Dai-kichi   great blessing    10%
    Chu-kichi   middle blessing   22%
    Sho-kichi   small blessing    20%
    Kichi       blessing          24%
    Sue-kichi   future blessing   14%
    Kyo         misfortune        10%

On a terminal the slip is drawn in a box with colours. When output goes to a pipe or a file it is plain text, ready for further processing. The kanji for the rank and the headings are only added with `-k`, because the font of the system console has none.

# OPTIONS
@ *-r*
    Draw a fresh slip at random instead of today's.
@ *-a*
    Plain ASCII: no box-drawing characters.
@ *-k*
    Add the kanji for the rank and for the headings. Use it on a terminal whose font has them, for example a terminal emulator on the serial line.

# FILES
@ /Hakurei/etc/motd
    Invites new arrivals to try it.

# NOTES
Tradition says that a bad slip should be tied to a pine branch at the shrine, so that the misfortune stays there. Tying it to the shell prompt does not work as well.

# EXAMPLES
    omikuji
    omikuji | head -3
    omikuji -r -a

# SEE ALSO
fortune(1), saisen(1), reimusay(1)
